somewhat” of a world record.  At the beginning of 1912, there was no official world record, as the " [:a {:href "/organizations/6"} "IAAF"] " did not start recognizing marks until 1913.  Until 1911, the best mark on record by an amateur was 31:40.0, by Walter George (GBR), set in London, on 28 July 1884, during a 12-mile handicap race.  The professional best had been set by Alfred Shrubb (GBR) in Glasgow, on 5 November 1904, during a one-hour race.  But on 16 November 1911, " [:a {:href "/athletes/68086"} "Jean Bouin"] " ran 30:58.8 at Colombes, France.  Bouin did not run the 10K at Stockholm, but finished a very close second to Hannes Kolehmainen in the " [:a {:href "/results/56634"} "5,000 metres"] "."] "\n\n" [:p {} "When the IAAF began to recognize world records, they thought that Bouin had run his mark in 1913.  They recognized only Kolehmainen’s winning Stockholm time as the initial IAAF-approved world record.  Bouin’s mark did not receive official recognition until 1926."] "\n"] [:table {:class "table table-striped"} [:thead {} [:tr {} [:th {} "Pos"] [:th {} "Competitor"] [:th {} "NOC"] [:th {} "R1"] [:th {} "Final"] [:th {}] [:th {}] [:th {}]]] [:tbody {} [:tr {:class "", :style ""} [:td {} "1"] [:td {} [:a {:href "/athletes/67641"} "Hannes Kolehmainen"]] [:td {} [:a {:href "/countries/FIN"} [:img {:style "padding-right: 2px; vertical-align: middle", :src "https://olympedia-flags.s3.eu-central-1.amazonaws.com/FIN.png"}] "FIN"]] [:td {} "33:49.0 (1 h1)"] [:td {} "31:20.8 (1)"] [:td {} [:span {:class "Gold"} "Gold"]] [:td {} [:span {:class "label label-default"} [:abbrev {:title "World Record"} "WR"]]] [:td {}]] [:tr {:class "", :style ""} [:td {} "2"] [:td {} [:a {:href "/athletes/79118"} "Lewis Tewanima"]] [:td {} [:a {:href "/countries/USA"} [:img {:style "padding-right: 2px; vertical-align: middle", :src "https://olympedia-flags.s3.eu-central-1.amazonaws.com/USA.png"}] "USA"]] [:td {} "32:31.4 (2 h2)"] [:td {} "32:06.6 (2)"] [:td {} [:span {:class "Silver"